+### Real speed without a frame rate target
+
+- **Symptom:** with the launcher's **Frame rate** at the game's own (`DM_FPS_TARGET=0`) a game runs in slow motion below 60 fps. The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time (Switch 2) at about 44 updates a second advanced its time manager 16.667 ms per update: 3.000 s of game time in 4.093 s of wall time. A frame rate target (`DM_FPS_TARGET=60`, the frame step) gives real time, but a late frame then steps two vsyncs, which judders on a device that holds 44-49 fps (iPad Pro M5, docked), where the game's own pacing is the smoothest.
+- **Cause:** the game's update converts the framework's integer FPS into its step, and its frame-rate controller leaves that FPS at 60 whatever the frames take (it never picks interval 2 on its own). The frame-ratio stub at the division (`main+0x6e6cc4`, `MATCH_FRAME_STEP`) gives the ratio the host's whole-vsync step only while a target is set and runs the game's division at target 0, so nothing told the game how long its frames really took.
+- **Fix:** profile key `fps_real on` (Ocarina of Time; `DM_FPS_REAL=1` / `0` overrides it). With no target, `freal_tick` (`hle_nvn.c`) measures the time between its presents, takes the mean of the last 8 (`nvn_freal_fps`, `nvn_fstep.h`: a game that presents on the display link's ticks jitters 1, 2, 1, 2 vsyncs at 45 fps, and the mean is what it steps evenly), clamps it to 1-4 vsyncs and writes the whole frame rate that matches (15..60) into the frame-step state page's +0x2c once per present. The frame-ratio stub (`patches.py` `frame_step`) divides the base rate by that FPS in place of the game's 60 and adds each step at +0x38 (units of 1/60000 s), which the host reads for its `real speed: game time … s in … s (… of real time)` line every 1,800 presents. Nothing is held and nothing is paced: the frames come as they come, and the game's clock follows them. A sample above 4 vsyncs (a load, a stall) enters the mean as 4, so a stall is not replayed; a short sample is not clamped, since frames that jitter 0.9 / 1.1 around a steady 60 average 1.0, and clamping them below one vsync would read 57 fps and run the game fast. The pieces degrade on their own: a main rewritten before the stub's target-0 path leaves +0x38 at 0 (the line says so), and a host without `fps_real` leaves +0x2c at 0, where the stub runs the game's division.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md#frame-rate).
+- **Check:** `dm/tools/tests/test_oot_frame_step.py` (unicorn: a measured 45 fps at +0x2c gives the ratio 60 / 45 and 60000 / 45 at +0x38, no measurement runs the division, a target ignores +0x2c); `make -C dm/gfx/tests GAME=0400486024DEC000 check_t_fstep` (`nvn_freal_fps`: steady, jittered, alternating 1 / 2 vsyncs, a stall in and out of the mean); on the Mac (measured with the earlier form of the stub, at the framework FPS load that feeds the same division), the game slowed with `DM_TEST_PRESENT_LAG=2,20` (20 ms more on every second present) and a probe on the time manager (`DM_PROBE=main:3b4e300@x0+10`, each call's ratio and wall time): with it on, the host's line read 1.000, 0.980, 0.995 and 0.960 of real time over four windows of 1,800 presents (present interval mean 1.26 to 1.89 vsyncs, fps fed 17 to 60), and the probe summed 92.17 s of game time in 95.83 s of wall time (0.962) over 18 to 46 updates a second, 0.999 in its steady 45 fps stretch, against 0.511 had the game kept 1/60 s per update (the dip is a stretch at 18 fps, where frames above 4 vsyncs enter the mean as 4); with `DM_FPS_REAL=0` under the same load, 1,698 updates of ratio 1.0 in 37.78 s: 0.749 of real time at 45 updates a second. Run notes: `ZEL_build/agents/speed.md`.
+
